content.text:
Now CSS for charge meter, perfect band, hint bar mobile, menu layouts. Let's carefully edit style.css.

1. Add charge meter styles after hint-bar. Desktop display only. We'll use:
```
.charge-meter { position:absolute; left:50%; bottom: calc(62px + var(--safe-b)); transform:translate(-50%, 8px); display:none; align-items:center; gap:8px; width:min(260px,46vw); padding:7px 12px; ... opacity:0; transition; pointer-events:none; z-index:... }
@media (pointer:fine) and (min-width:901px){ .charge-meter{display:flex} }
.charge-meter.is-on { opacity:1; transform:translate(-50%,0); }
.charge-label { font-size:6.5px; ... color muted}
.charge-track { position:relative; flex:1; height:7px; border-radius:4px; background: rgba(255,255,255,.12); overflow:hidden? Need needle overflow visible. }
```
If overflow hidden, needle is clipped at edges but fine. The perfect zone inside. Use:
```
.charge-track { position:relative; flex:1; height:8px; border-radius:999px; background:rgba(255,255,255,.11); box-shadow: inset 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.5); }
.charge-zone { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:var(--perfect-a,70%); right:calc(100% - var(--perfect-b,90%)); background:linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(215,255,74,.25), rgba(215,255,74,.65), rgba(215,255,74,.25)); border-left:1px solid rgba(215,255,74,.7); border-right:1px solid ...; }
.charge-fill { position:absolute; left:0; top:0; bottom:0; width:0; border-radius:inherit; background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--cyan),var(--lime)); box-shadow:0 0 10px rgba(215,255,74,.4); transition:width .04s linear; }
.charge-needle { position:absolute; top:-2px; bottom:-2px; width:2px; left:0; background:#fff; box-shadow:0 0 8px #fff; transform:translateX(-1px); transition:left .04s linear; }
.charge-perfect { font-size:6px; letter-spacing:.14em; color:var(--lime); opacity:0; }
.charge-meter.is-perfect .charge-perfect { opacity:1; }
.charge-meter.is-perfect { border-color: rgba(215,255,74,.6); box-shadow: 0 0 30px rgba(215,255,74,.18); }
```
Wait the `charge-zone` using `right: calc(100% - var(--perfect-b))` is fine. Or use width calc. Good.

2. Shoot button ::after perfect band. Add:
```
.tbtn-shoot::after {
 content:''; position:absolute; inset:-5px; border-radius:50%;
 background: conic-gradient(from -90deg, transparent 0 0.69turn, rgba(215,255,74,.5) 0.69turn 0.9turn, transparent 0.9turn);
 -webkit-mask: radial-gradient(farthest-side, transparent calc(100% - 3px), #000 calc(100% - 2px));
 mask: ...;
 opacity:.55; transition: opacity .12s;
}
```
The band should reflect sniper's 64-94; CSS can't know. We could set a CSS var `--perfect-start` on the button from UI.setStyle. Let's set both `--perfect-a` and `--perfect-b` on the shoot button too (as turns). In UI.setStyle, set `--perfect-a` on chargeMeter; also on shootBtn set `--pf-a` and `--pf-b` in turns:
```
const pmin = perfect[0]/100, pmax = perfect[1]/100;
btn.style.setProperty('--perfect-a-turn', `${pmin}turn`) ...
```
Simpler: set `--perfect-a` and `--perfect-b` on the root style? We can set on `document.documentElement` so both meter and button use percentages/turns? For conic gradient we need turns. Use `--perfect-start: 0.7turn` and `--perfect-end: 0.9turn` on the button. In setStyle:
```
this.el.shootBtn?.style.setProperty('--perfect-start', `${perfect[0]/100}turn`);
this.el.shootBtn?.style.setProperty('--perfect-end', `${perfect[1]/100}turn`);
```
Then CSS:
```
background: conic-gradient(from -90deg, transparent 0 var(--perfect-start, 0.7turn), rgba(...) var(--perfect-start,.7turn) var(--perfect-end,.9turn), transparent var(--perfect-end,.9turn));
```
Note the conic starts from -90deg; the perfect zone 70-90% of the turn corresponds to the later part. Good.
3. Hint bar mobile display.
4. Menu changes.
5. Landscape compact layout.
6. Maybe `.hud-side` hidden on all mobile already.
7. `menu-grid` margin auto.

Let's patch CSS in chunks.

